---
/**
 * K Culture Wire — **들어온 주가 꼭대기였나.** (`/climb`)
 *
 * ── ⛔ 이 지면이 지키는 것 ────────────────────────────────────
 * ⛔ 「입소문」이라 쓰지 않는다. 우리가 본 것은 **순위가 올랐다**는 것뿐이다.
 *    순위는 내가 올라도 오르고 위엣것이 내려도 오른다. 그 둘을 우리는 못 가른다.
 * ⛔ 대조군을 표로 낸다. 「오래 머물면 오를 기회도 많다」를 지면이 스스로 시험한다.
 * ⛔ 얼마나 높이 올랐나는 안 잰다. 10위 → 9위와 10위 → 1위가 여기서는 똑같다.
 * ⛔ 광고 자리를 만들지 않았다. Riot Production(App 866800) 승인 전이다.
 *
 * ⚠ 수를 여기 손으로 적지 않는다. 8/8 에 라이브 날짜가 하루 일렀던 까닭이 그것이다.
 */
import WikiTip from '../../layouts/WikiTip.astro';
import KcwRelatedArticles from '../../components/KcwRelatedArticles.astro';
import data from '../../data/wikitip-climb.json';

const TITLE = 'Does a title climb after it enters the chart?';
const 한 = data.korean;
const 밖 = data.other;
const DESC =
  `Across ${data.runs.toLocaleString('en-US')} chart runs, ${한.climbedPc}% of Korean runs reach a `
  + `higher rank than their opening week against ${밖.climbedPc}% for everything else — and the gap `
  + 'holds inside every run-length band.';

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Dataset',
  name: TITLE,
  description: DESC,
  creator: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'K Culture Wire' },
  isBasedOn: ['https://www.netflix.com/tudum/top10', 'https://query.wikidata.org'],
};
---

<WikiTip title={TITLE} description={DESC} jsonLd={jsonLd} canonical="https://www.kculturewire.com/climb">
  <h1>{TITLE}</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Netflix publishes a rank per title, per country, per week. That makes one question answerable
    for every run on the chart: was the opening week the highest it ever sat?
  </p>
  <p class="q">{data.question}</p>

  <section>
    <h2>{data.runs.toLocaleString('en-US')} runs</h2>
    <p class="note"><b>How a run is counted.</b> {data.unit}</p>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>&nbsp;</th><th>Runs</th><th>Climbed after week one</th><th>Peaked in week one</th><th>Mean weeks</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr class="edge">
            <td class="nm">Korean titles</td>
            <td>{한.runs.toLocaleString('en-US')}</td>
            <td class="tot">{한.climbedPc}%</td><td>{한.peakedFirstWeekPc}%</td><td>{한.meanWeeks}</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="nm">Everything else</td>
            <td>{밖.runs.toLocaleString('en-US')}</td>
            <td class="tot">{밖.climbedPc}%</td><td>{밖.peakedFirstWeekPc}%</td><td>{밖.meanWeeks}</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      <b>Runs of a single week are not in this table.</b> {data.minWeeksWhy}
      {' '}The gap between the two rows is <b>{data.gap} points</b>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The objection, tested</h2>
    <p class="warn">
      Korean runs last longer — {한.meanWeeks} weeks against {밖.meanWeeks} — and a longer run has
      more weeks in which to beat its first. If that is all this is, the gap should vanish once runs
      of the same length are compared.
    </p>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Run length</th><th>Korean climbed</th><th>Everything else</th><th>Gap</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {data.bands.map((b) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="nm">{b.label}</td>
              <td>{b.korean.climbedPc}% <span class="pc">n={b.korean.runs.toLocaleString('en-US')}</span></td>
              <td>{b.other.climbedPc}% <span class="pc">n={b.other.runs.toLocaleString('en-US')}</span></td>
              <td class="tot">{b.gap > 0 ? '+' : ''}{b.gap}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      <b>{data.survivesLengthControl ? 'It does not vanish in any band.' : 'It vanishes in at least one band — see the row above.'}</b>
      {' '}The gap runs from {Math.min(...data.bands.map((b) => b.gap))} points to
      {' '}{Math.max(...data.bands.map((b) => b.gap))}, and among the longer runs it widens rather
      than narrows.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>And by format</h2>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Format</th><th>Korean climbed</th><th>Everything else</th><th>Gap</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {data.formats.map((f) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="nm">{f.format}</td>
              <td>{f.korean.climbedPc}% <span class="pc">n={f.korean.runs.toLocaleString('en-US')}</span></td>
              <td>{f.other.climbedPc}% <span class="pc">n={f.other.runs.toLocaleString('en-US')}</span></td>
              <td class="tot">{f.gap > 0 ? '+' : ''}{f.gap}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      Korean chart presence is more heavily series than the field is, so the two formats are
      separated here. Korean films climb less often than Korean series and still more often than
      non-Korean films — <b>{data.survivesFormatControl ? 'the gap survives in both formats' : 'the gap does not survive in one of the formats, shown above'}</b>.
      {' '}<a href="/article/korean-titles-climb">The full story &rarr;</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <KcwRelatedArticles page="/climb" />

  <section class="src">
    <h2>Where these numbers come from, and what they cannot answer</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Netflix</b> — {data.source}</li>
      <li>
        <b>Not word of mouth.</b> A rank improves when a title gains viewers <em>or</em> when the
        titles above it lose them. Nothing here separates those, and we do not use the phrase as a
        finding.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Not how far.</b> We measured whether a later week beat the opening week, not by how much.
        Tenth to ninth counts the same as tenth to first.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Not viewing.</b> A rank is a position against the other titles in that country that week.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>The two columns are complements.</b> Climbed and peaked-in-week-one sum to the number of
        runs in every group above, and the collector refuses to run if they do not.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>One market is excluded and we name it.</b> {data.excludedCountry.name} —
        {' '}{data.excludedCountry.why}
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <p class="back"><a href="/">&larr; Back to the front page</a></p>
</WikiTip>

<style>
  h1 { margin: 0 0 .5rem; font-size: 1.7rem; letter-spacing: -.02em; }
  .lead { margin: 0 0 .6rem; font-size: 1rem; color: var(--muted); }
  .q { margin: 0 0 2rem; font-size: .9rem; color: var(--accent); }
  section { margin: 0 0 2.5rem; }
  h2 { font-size: 1.1rem; margin: 0 0 .7rem; letter-spacing: -.01em; }
  .note { font-size: .82rem; color: var(--muted); margin: .9rem 0 0; line-height: 1.6; }
  .note b { color: var(--ink); }
  .warn {
    font-size: .86rem; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 1rem;
    padding: .8rem .9rem; border-left: 3px solid var(--accent);
    background: var(--card); border-radius: 0 6px 6px 0;
  }
  table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: .85rem; }
  th, td { padding: .45rem .55rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); text-align: right;
           white-space: nowrap; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
  th { font-size: .72rem; letter-spacing: .03em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--muted);
       font-weight: 700; }
  th:first-child, td.nm { text-align: left; white-space: normal; }
  td.tot { font-weight: 700; }
  .pc { color: var(--muted); font-size: .76rem; }
  tr.edge td { background: var(--card); }
  @media (max-width: 480px) {
    table { font-size: .78rem; }
    th, td { padding: .38rem .28rem; }
    .pc { display: block; }
  }
  .src ul { margin: 0; padding-left: 1.1rem; font-size: .85rem; color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.7; }
  .src li { margin: .45rem 0; }
  .src b { color: var(--ink); }
  .back { margin-top: 2.5rem; font-size: .9rem; }
</style>
